AB  - The rapid growth of Virtual Reality (VR), Augmented Reality (AR), and Metaverse platforms demands highly personalized experiences in immersive 3D environments, yet existing recommendation methods struggle to exploit rich spatial context, multimodal interaction signals, and strict real-time latency requirements. We propose LLM-VR, a unified framework that integrates large language models with VR/AR systems to enable context-aware ranking and recommendation. LLM-VR encodes spatial, temporal, semantic, and biometric cues through specialized embeddings and fuses them using a multimodal context engine with hierarchical attention over features, contexts, and items. To handle conflicting or ambiguous sensor signals that frequently arise from occlusion, motion blur, or tracking loss, we introduce an uncertainty-aware attention (UAA) module that quantifies per-modality aleatoric uncertainty and routes low-confidence observations through a coupled visual grounding network (VGN), preventing error propagation in complex immersive scenes. To promote equitable recommendations across diverse user populations, we further incorporate a fairness-aware training pipeline combining demographic-conditioned mixup augmentation with gradient-reversal adversarial de-biasing, reducing demographic parity difference by 56 % relative to unmitigated training. To support low-latency inference, LLM-VR employs a real-time optimization pipeline combining 8-bit dynamic quantization, 3D spatial indexing, and adaptive batching. We provide hardware-stratified latency profiling across three representative headset tiers—entry-level (Meta Quest 2), mid-range (HTC Vive Pro 2), and enterprise (Microsoft HoloLens 2)—confirming sub500 ms feasibility on all tested devices. Cross-platform evaluation on the publicly available VRRec-2023 benchmark validates generalization beyond our proprietary environments. Experiments on three heterogeneous VR platforms with 2,847 users demonstrate that LLM-VR improves user engagement by 23.4 % over strong baselines, achieves 91.2 % accuracy for virtual object placement and 87.8 % for experience recommendation, and answers contextual queries within 340 ms on average, meeting sub- 500 ms interactive constraints.
