AB  - Consumers with health limitations increasingly require nutritional information tailored to their personal context. However, conventional packaging labels remain standardized and inflexible. This research therefore proposes a domain-specific tool-augmented LLM-based health agent that perceives food packaging via AR, reasons over user health profiles using rule-based scoring and LLM prompt engineering, and acts by delivering personalized health guidance through AR overlay and voice output. The agent is developed and evaluated by integrating augmented reality with an LLM-based recommendation system and rule-based health scoring within a 6-Layer agent architecture and evaluated by 30 participants (15 with health limitations, 15 without). Evaluation using SUS, TAM, and NPS revealed that the system achieved a usability score of 75.08 with a standard deviation of 4.62 (classified as good), both groups perceived a high level of usefulness mean score of 4.28, and overall recommendation intention was highly rated with an NPS of 8.30 out of 10, demonstrating that the proposed health agent can effectively support personalized and health-appropriate product decisions.
