AB  - Annotations play a critical role in extended reality (XR) for collaboration, training, and information delivery. However, existing approaches often suffer from fragmented multimodal inputs, redundant interaction steps, and high user task load. To address these challenges, we propose a multimodal interaction method based on large language models (LLMs) that integrates gesture and voice inputs. Our method leverages the reasoning capabilities of LLMs to synchronize users’ gesture-based operations with voice commands in real time, enabling the automatic generation of spatially anchored annotations while minimizing manual steps. To validate its effectiveness, we conducted two user studies with 16 participants performing annotation tasks using both traditional multimodal methods and our proposed approach. Experimental results demonstrate that our method reduces task completion time, decreases NASA-TLX task load scores, and improves operational coherence based on subjective user feedback.
