AB  - Autistic individuals often experience sensory hypersensitivity, making immersive virtual environments potentially overwhelming without adaptive regulation. This work presents a novel adaptive virtual reality (AVR) framework that dynamically adjusts environmental stimuli based on real-time physiological indicators, supported by lightweight large language models (LLMs). The system combines (1) heart rate driven sensory modulation, (2) an Unreal Engine therapeutic scenario with fully parameterized lighting and sound controls, and (3) an AI-driven conversational agent capable of delivering calming guidance and interactive support. A heart rate classification pipeline, implemented using gpt-4.1-nano-2025-04-14, transitions the environment between overstimulating (red, loud soundscape) and calming (blue, reduced volume) profiles, while a unified LLM prompt generates brief single-paragraph relaxation cues during heightened arousal. A second pipeline integrates speech-to-text, LLM reasoning, and text-to-speech to enable continuous natural interaction inside VR. Early testing with simulated physiological data demonstrates the system's ability to detect stress patterns, adjust sensory variables in real time, and produce coherent therapeutic responses. Although full user validation and integration with real physiological sensors remain ongoing, these results indicate that coupling VR with LLM-driven adaptive control offers a promising foundation for accessible, personalized, and regulation-supportive immersive environments for autistic users.
