AB  - The integration of Generative AI into virtual reality (VR) training environments presents a transformative opportunity for procedural skill development across a wide range of domains, including engineering, science, and medicine. This work explores a methodology that leverages large language models (LLMs) to automatically assess learner performance and provide formative feedback during VR-based procedural simulations. By analyzing user actions within the virtual environment, the system identifies deviations from correct procedure and generates real-time feedback to support skill acquisition and adaptive learning. We demonstrate the methodology through cervical spine surgery training, presenting how the system assesses and gives feedback to learners’ actions in VR procedural training. While the work is situated in a surgical context, the underlying approach is designed to be domain-agnostic, enabling adoption across various procedural training scenarios. An exploratory user study reveals that while the feedback provided by LLM is effective for assessment, introducing actionable items and including domain-specific terms can further reduce the gap between LLM-generated feedback and feedback given by a domain expert.
