AB  - The preservation and dissemination of cultural heritage in modern society rely heavily on the accessibility of artistic knowledge; however, traditional museum experiences often remain passive, limiting visitors to visual appreciation without offering insight into the procedural complexities of artistic creation. Static exhibitions fail to capture the temporal evolution and decision-making processes required to construct a masterpiece. To address this gap, we propose “AIrtist,” an immersive Virtual Reality (VR) framework designed to bridge art appreciation and interactive engagement. Our system leverages the Meta Oculus Quest 3 to immerse users in a reconstruction of an artist's studio, specifically focusing on the works of Vincent van Gogh. The methodology integrates a modular pipeline: a theoretical learning phase driven by Large Language Models (LLMs) and ElevenLabs Text-to-Speech (TTS) for contextual narration, and a practical gamified painting phase grounded in Deep Reinforcement Learning (DRL). We utilize the “Learning to Paint” model to decompose images into stroke sequences. To translate these algorithmic outputs into human-interpretable guides, we developed a novel stroke analysis engine that employs Principal Component Analysis (PCA) and connected component labeling on differential frame masks to extract geometric stroke vectors. Qualitative results demonstrate that our pipeline successfully decomposes texture-rich paintings into coherent, interactive steps, allowing users to experience the construction of complex artistic styles within the virtual environment.
ER  - 

