AB  - This study investigates limitations in the cross-cultural non-verbal behavior understanding of large language models (LLM) within embodied virtual agents on Metaverse social platforms. A dedicated high-conflict benchmark, A dedicated high-conflict benchmark, Cross-Cultural Non-Verbal Behavior mini (CC-NVB-mini), comprising 100 rigorously annotated samples, is introduced and made publicly available. Systematic evaluation is conducted on four contemporary models :Gemini 1.5 Flash, Grok-4, ChatGPT-4, DeepSeek-V3, under Zero-Shot, Few-Shot, and Cross-Cultural Prompting (CCP) conditions. In the Zero-Shot setting, the models yield an average score of 5.92, substantially below the human baseline of 7.85. Introduction of the parameter-free, example-free CCP strategy raises the average score to 8.22, exceeding the human reference by 0.37 points, with Grok-4 reaching 8.74. These results indicate that cross-cultural competence is present in latent form within current LLMs, and that performance bottlenecks arise primarily from activation rather than absence of relevant knowledge. The CCP approach offers a lightweight, real-time deployable mechanism for cultural-conflict mitigation in embodied avatars. The work aligns closely with the EI-OAHV 2026 focus on Embodied Intelligence by providing an activation-based engineering solution for cross-cultural non-verbal understanding, supporting safer and more inclusive human-embodied interaction in multicultural virtual-physical environments.
ER  - 

