AB  - The Miracle Question (MQ) is a core technique in Solution-Focused Brief Therapy (SFBT) that guides individuals to envision a future in which their current problems no longer exist, supporting goal-oriented reflection and action planning. In practice, however, MQ relies heavily on users’ imagination and verbal articulation, which can limit engagement and increase cognitive load. To address this limitation, we present a virtual reality (VR)-based embodied counseling system that operationalizes the structured MQ process within an immersive, non-anthropomorphic environment. The system formalizes MQ as a script-constrained, state-aware dialogue model, in which predefined dialogue states govern the counseling flow while a large language model is used solely for natural language generation. Instead of employing a humanoid virtual agent, the system adopts a nature-inspired interaction metaphor designed to minimize social evaluative cues and support self-projection. A key design element is an embodied miracle moment, in which the imagined future state is externalized as a symbolic, multisensory VR event, transforming abstract verbal imagination into a perceptible embodied experience. In a pilot prepost evaluation, participants showed increased positive affect and reduced negative affect after a single MQ-guided VR session. In a separate between-subject comparison against a humanoid-agent-based presentation, the non-anthropomorphic condition yielded higher perceived psychological safety and greater self-disclosure. These results suggest that VR can provide an effective embodied pathway for structured reflective counseling, demonstrating how controlled dialogue flow and environment-driven embodiment can be integrated in automated reflective systems without real-time human intervention.
ER  - 

